# Line endings for files whose BYTES are compared, not just their content.
#
# keys/minisign-trusted.txt is read by CMake at configure time and embedded in the
# binary as a raw string literal. A raw string literal maps a CRLF in the source to a
# single newline during translation, so what is compiled in is LF on every platform no
# matter what is on disk. Git's autocrlf, which is on by default in a Windows checkout,
# gives that same file CRLF in the working tree - and the two then differ.
#
# That difference is harmless to verification: the parser tolerates both, and a Windows
# build carries the right key. It is not harmless to the check. The binary is supposed
# to carry the keyring THIS repository holds, byte for byte, and the test asserting so
# is the only thing standing between a release and a verifier trusting a key nobody
# reviewed. Normalising the comparison to make it pass would delete exactly the property
# worth having, so the file is pinned instead.
#
# Found by the first run of the Windows test job, on the first pull request that had one.
keys/minisign-trusted.txt text eol=lf
